    note: base runs the latency arm and then the loss arm back to back inside one record, with no proxifier loaded: it is the harness floor.
    note: base is two 5 s phases, so parameters.seconds is their sum and parameters.latency/parameters.loss carry the effective per-phase parameters; metrics.elapsedSeconds is the measured wall time.
    note: both phases take every load parameter the plan's BASE entry declares and fall back per phase only where it declares none.
    note: the latency phase runs the plan's protocol (tcp+udp) and the loss phase always runs udp, so a BASE entry that wants a udp latency floor has to declare protocol.
    note: latency is measured from each request's intended send instant, never from the actual send instant.
    note: sendWouldBlock counts sends the kernel did not accept synchronously; those sends still complete, so they are reported but not folded into clientSendLoss.
    note: a reply carrying another flow's connection id validates against that flow's own filler, so it is rejected by connection id before the filler check and booked as foreignConnection; only a reply that carries this socket's own id and fails its filler is booked as corrupt.
    note: tcp.connectAttempts counts every TCP connect the arm made -- the per-lane lane connects plus the 1 Hz connect probe -- and tcp.connectFailures counts the attempts in that same population that failed, so tcp.meanConnectMs is the mean connect duration over its successful attempts and is null when there were none; latency.tcp-connect separately holds the probe's own samples, measured from each probe's intended instant.
    note: a request offered at a full in-flight window is deferred, not dropped: it goes out as soon as a reply frees a slot, still stamped with its original intended instant, so a stalled product is published as an inflated sample instead of a missing one; windowOverflow counts those deferrals.
    note: the deferred queue holds at most 256 request(s) per lane, i.e. 10 s of the offered rate capped at 1048576; a request that bound discards is counted in backlogDrops and folded into gates.clientSendLoss, so an arm that censors its own tail cannot publish a clean gate.
    note: sendFailures counts individual sends that threw; the offer loop continues after one, so a transient error costs that request's sample instead of the rest of the schedule, and that sample is folded into gates.clientSendLoss.
    note: each lane drains its receive side for up to 1 s after the offer loop so the cohort still in flight is sampled rather than discarded; outstandingAtTeardown counts what was still unanswered, plus any deferred request that never got a slot, when the bound expired.
    note: tcp and udp counters are summed only after every lane has joined, so sentOk cannot exceed supplied however many lanes a plan asks for.
    note: in-flight ceiling (tcp): 1 lane(s) at 20.193 requests/s aggregate achieved with inFlightWindow 64 per lane put the measurable direct-latency ceiling at 3169.4 ms; slower requests are measured through the deferred queue, and the ceiling was not reached (windowOverflow = 0).
    note: in-flight ceiling (udp): 1 lane at 20.193 requests/s achieved with inFlightWindow 64 put the measurable direct-latency ceiling at 3169.4 ms; slower requests are measured through the deferred queue, and the ceiling was not reached (windowOverflow = 0).
    note: lanes actually run: tcp 1 of 1, udp 1 of 1; per-lane supplied and sent counts are published as tcp.laneSupplied and tcp.laneSentOk.
    note: W is the plan's lossWindowMs, 200 ms when the plan does not declare one: it is declared and published as metrics.window rather than derived, so every row's arrived/late/never split is reproducible from the record alone (RFC 2680 style, a datagram is lost if it has not arrived W after its intended send instant).
    note: a slot leaves the in-flight window W after its intended send instant even without an arrival, so a datagram the path drops cannot wedge the offer loop and stop the client sending.
    note: every sent datagram is classified exactly once, so arrived + late + never + abandonedAtTeardown + corruptDatagrams == sent by construction; corruptDatagrams counts the sent datagrams booked corrupt, while corrupt counts corrupt arrivals and so also counts replay of a frame already booked corrupt.
    note: the drain runs until W after the last real send, so no datagram is called lost before its whole W has passed; a drain cut short leaves the datagrams still inside their window in abandonedAtTeardown rather than in never, and they are excluded from lossRate.
    note: reordered counts arrivals that follow the arrival of a higher sequence, the RFC 4737 definition; arriving after a higher sequence was merely sent does not count.
    note: corrupt frames whose header decoded are removed from the never bucket; the strict-loss rate adds corrupt back on top of loss, as RFC 2680 requires.
    note: a reply carrying another flow's connection id validates against that flow's own filler, so it is rejected by connection id before the filler check and booked as foreignConnection; a reply for a sequence this socket never sent is booked as unmatchedReplies.
    note: sendWouldBlock counts sends the kernel did not accept synchronously; a send that failed synchronously is counted in sendFailures only, because it completes rather than blocks and never enters the sent population.
    note: outOfRangeSequences counts sequences the tracker refused as outside its bounded sequence space; a non-zero value means part of the offered schedule was never tracked, so this record's classification covers fewer datagrams than sent and must not be read as a complete loss measurement.
    note: the offer phase and the drain phase share one socket, so a reply is never delivered to a socket nobody reads.
    note: a rate or ratio whose denominator is zero is written as null rather than 0: nothing was sent, so there is no rate to report.
